	abstract = {Navigating the intricacies of scheduling in a blocked hybrid flow shop with stringent time constraints is a complex task, especially when considering the trade-offs among product exposure time, production efficiency, and energy consumption. To tackle this challenge, a multi-objective optimization model is developed, incorporating three key objectives: minimizing product exposure time, maximum completion time, and total energy use. To solve the corresponding scheduling problem efficiently, a novel Multi-Objective Evolution Cooperation Artificial Bee Colony (MECABC) Algorithm is developed. It integrates three heterogeneous optimizers via a collaborative evolution mechanism and an integrated learning strategy In addition, a local neighbourhood-based solution generation method is introduced to mitigate the blocking. Moreover, a fuzzy decision-making strategy is employed to select the most satisfactory scheduling scheme. Simulation experiments on benchmark and real-world production scenarios demonstrate that the method significantly outperforms existing methods. The approach is further validated in a sausage production workshop, confirming its effectiveness. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
